-- An @nlg on a MIXFIX definition, read at a call site in ANOTHER module.
-- The definitions are in nlg-mixfix-import-lib.l4; the calls are the same, row
-- for row, as nlg-mixfix-local.l4's, so the two outputs can be read side by
-- side. See that file for what each row is.

IMPORT `nlg-mixfix-import-lib`

#EVAL `the lower of` 840 `and` 160
#EVAL `the amount by which` 840 `exceeds` 160
#EVAL `the shortfall of` 160 `against` 840
#EVAL 840 `plus` 160
#EVAL `the first of` 840 `then` 160
#EVAL `the bare` 840 `and` 160
#EVAL `the greater` 3 4

#EVAL `the lower of` (`the lower of` 5 `and` 6) `and` 7

#EVAL `the lower of` OF 840, 160
#EVAL `the amount by which` OF 840, 160
#EVAL `the lower of` WITH a IS 840, b IS 160
#EVAL `the amount by which` WITH a IS 840, b IS 160

#EVAL `the gap` 840 160
#EVAL `the gap` WITH a IS 840, b IS 160

-- Render rows. `l4 nlg` prints directives only, so these add nothing to the
-- output above; they give `l4 render` a call to each function inside the body
-- of another rule, which is the other place a herald is read at a call site.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render lower` n MEANS `the lower of` n `and` 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render exceeds` n MEANS `the amount by which` n `exceeds` 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render shortfall` n MEANS `the shortfall of` n `against` 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render plus` n MEANS n `plus` 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render first` n MEANS `the first of` n `then` 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render bare` n MEANS `the bare` n `and` 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render greater` n MEANS `the greater` n 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render nested` n MEANS `the lower of` (`the lower of` n `and` 100) `and` 200

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render of` n MEANS `the lower of` OF n, 100

-- A WITH call inside another rule's body. The herald of `the amount by which`
-- and `the gap` follows their last input, so it used to be read a second time as
-- the label of that input; the herald of `the lower of` sits above its head and
-- never was.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render exceeds with` n MEANS (`the amount by which` WITH a IS n, b IS 100) PLUS 1

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render gap with` n MEANS (`the gap` WITH a IS n, b IS 100) PLUS 1

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render lower with` n MEANS (`the lower of` WITH a IS n, b IS 100) PLUS 1

-- A function used as a projection's selector keeps its plain name: "the span's
-- size's half", not "the span's size's half of x". A herald that follows the
-- input is moved onto the rule before a call is read (see `render exceeds with`
-- above), which gives the name a herald; only a call should read it.
DECLARE Span HAS size IS A NUMBER

GIVEN s IS A Span
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render half selector` s MEANS s's size's half

GIVEN s IS A Span
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render third selector` s MEANS s's size's third

-- A WITH call as the WHOLE body of a rule. It is a call, not a record
-- construction, so it reads as the function's herald with the arguments in a
-- where clause, as `l4 nlg` prints it, and not as "is a the gap with" and a list
-- of fields. `Pt`, a record that carries a herald of its own, is the control: its
-- literal is still a record literal.
DECLARE Pt @nlg a point
  HAS x IS A NUMBER
      y IS A NUMBER

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render exceeds direct` n MEANS `the amount by which` WITH a IS n, b IS 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render gap direct` n MEANS `the gap` WITH a IS n, b IS 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`render lower direct` n MEANS `the lower of` WITH a IS n, b IS 100

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Pt
`render point` n MEANS Pt WITH x IS n, y IS 100
